# RankNova Production-Ready Web App Research and Google AI Studio Master Prompt

## Executive summary

**RankNova** is technically viable as a focused three-tool utility website built in Google AI Studio Build mode. Google AI Studio currently supports full-stack web apps with a React frontend, Node.js server runtime, server-side secret handling, Firebase Authentication/Firestore integration, GitHub two-way sync, and direct deployment to Cloud Run. That makes it suitable for a real production implementation rather than only a visual prototype. citeturn430749search0turn696138search4turn696138search3

The recommended launch scope is exactly the three tools you specified: **QR Code Generator, PDF Compressor, and AI Background Remover**. Existing products demonstrate sustained utility around these workflows: Adobe Express offers QR generation with customization and downloadable formats; iLovePDF offers multiple PDF compression levels; and remove.bg operates a dedicated automated background-removal service. These references demonstrate mature user use cases, but they do **not** prove that RankNova will rank automatically or that any keyword has low SEO competition. citeturn406183search1turn406183search3turn406183search2

There are two important implementation corrections. First, a QR code containing a URL should simply encode the URL locally; the server should **not fetch that URL**, because doing so adds unnecessary SSRF exposure. OWASP specifically identifies user-controlled remote URLs as a common SSRF vector and recommends strong URL/IP controls whenever server-side fetching exists. citeturn536294search8 Second, the default transparent background-removal download should be **PNG**. PNG explicitly supports alpha transparency. A conventional JPEG export should flatten the cutout onto a selected solid background rather than claim to preserve transparent pixels. citeturn559674search0turn559674search17

For background removal, the safest commercial architecture is to make the processing provider configurable. A hosted API such as remove.bg is operationally simple, whereas self-hosted/open-source models require careful license review. For example, `rembg` itself is MIT-licensed, but its documentation warns that individual model weights can have separate licenses; its current default BRIA model requires a commercial agreement for commercial use. IMG.LY's browser background-removal package is AGPL unless another license is obtained. This is exactly why RankNova should never assume that “open source code” automatically means every bundled model is commercially unrestricted. citeturn914894search5turn914894search7turn914894search8turn536294search3

For PDF processing, the product should use a **client-first / server-fallback** design. Lightweight browser-side optimizations can avoid uploading documents, while difficult PDFs can be sent to an isolated server worker after explicit user action. A production pipeline must honestly report the before/after file size and must never claim compression when the output is not actually smaller. `pdfcpu`, for example, provides PDF validation and optimization under Apache 2.0, but its “optimize” operation primarily targets redundant PDF resources; a true quality slider that aggressively downsamples embedded images requires an appropriately licensed processing pipeline rather than a fake percentage control. citeturn536294search0turn536294search1turn536294search2

AdSense readiness should be designed from day one but **ads should remain disabled until the site is accepted and publisher IDs are configured**. Google prohibits artificial clicks/impressions, misleading ad placement, encouraging users to interact with ads, and layouts in which ads can be confused with navigation or download controls. Google also requires a certified CMP integrated with the IAB TCF for personalized ads served through its publisher products to users in the EEA, UK, and Switzerland. citeturn696138search0turn696138search1turn696138search2turn817648search0

Legal compliance cannot be reduced to generating a Privacy Policy template. GDPR principles include purpose limitation, data minimization, storage limitation, security, transparency, and privacy by design/default. California's CCPA applies only when statutory thresholds and conditions are met, but covered businesses must provide relevant privacy rights. COPPA creates additional requirements if a service is directed to children under 13 or has actual knowledge that it collects their personal information. These requirements vary by jurisdiction, so the application should implement privacy-oriented defaults and treat generated legal text as a starting point for legal review, not a guarantee of compliance. citeturn832256search0turn832256search2turn832256search4

The keyword recommendations below are therefore **priority keyword candidates**, not verified “low-competition” claims. Google Keyword Planner itself provides estimated monthly searches and advertiser competition, and Google notes that shorter/general keywords can face stronger competition. Validate the final targets in Keyword Planner for the countries RankNova intends to target before publishing the landing pages. citeturn666021search0turn666021search2turn666021search10

## Product architecture and implementation decisions

RankNova should use public, crawlable landing pages plus an application layer for actual tool execution. Google states that pages need to be accessible to Googlebot, return successful responses, and contain indexable content to be eligible for indexing; eligibility still does not guarantee indexing. Google also documents sitemaps, canonicalization, metadata, logical URLs, and JavaScript considerations as part of crawl/index management. citeturn817648search3turn817648search4turn817648search5

The public information architecture should be:

| Route | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `/` | RankNova homepage |
| `/qr-code-generator` | QR Code Generator |
| `/compress-pdf` | PDF Compressor |
| `/background-remover` | AI Background Remover |
| `/blog` | Original educational content |
| `/blog/[slug]` | Individual article |
| `/about` | Business and service explanation |
| `/contact` | Contact/support |
| `/privacy-policy` | Privacy practices |
| `/terms` | Terms of use |
| `/copyright-dmca` | Copyright and takedown procedure |
| `/acceptable-use` | User-content and abuse rules |
| `/admin` | Owner-only dashboard, noindex |

The homepage should promote **only these three tools at launch**. Blog posts, legal pages, an admin panel, analytics, and CMS functionality are supporting infrastructure, not additional public tools.

Google AI Studio's current full-stack environment already provides a React frontend and Node.js server runtime, supports npm packages and server-side secrets, can provision Firestore and Firebase Authentication, and supports GitHub synchronization. Production deployment can create a Cloud Run service. citeturn696138search4turn430749search0turn696138search3

For a custom domain, Google Cloud currently recommends putting a **global external Application Load Balancer** in front of Cloud Run; direct Cloud Run domain mapping is described as limited-availability/preview, while Firebase Hosting is another supported option. The load-balancer approach also enables Cloud CDN and Cloud Armor if those become useful later. citeturn430749search3

### Tool implementation choices

**QR Code Generator:** perform QR generation in-browser wherever practical. Support URL, plain text, Wi-Fi, and vCard payloads; custom dimensions; error-correction setting; foreground/background colors; and PNG/SVG export. The user-entered URL should be encoded, not fetched. Adobe's current QR tool similarly exposes customization and downloadable formats, confirming the workflow is understandable to mainstream users. citeturn406183search1turn406183search7

**PDF Compressor:** attempt browser-side processing first and clearly tell users when their document remains on-device. When the selected mode requires server processing, ask for confirmation before upload. Server-side uploads should use strict file-type/signature validation, size limits, generated temporary filenames, resource limits, isolated processing, and immediate cleanup. OWASP recommends extension allowlisting, independent MIME/signature validation, filename controls, upload size limits, storage isolation, secure libraries, and antivirus/sandboxing where appropriate. citeturn536294search9

**AI Background Remover:** allow JPG, JPEG, PNG, and optionally WebP input, validate actual file content, strip unnecessary metadata from output, and create a transparent PNG. JPEG output should require the user to select a white, black, or custom solid background. remove.bg demonstrates that background removal is commonly exposed as both an interactive tool and an API, while open-source alternatives such as `rembg` can run locally but require per-model license review. citeturn406183search2turn406183search8turn914894search5

A commercially deployable open-source choice is still **unspecified** until you perform license due diligence on the exact model, weights, training-data terms, and dependencies you intend to ship. This is important because current repositories show real licensing complexity: IMG.LY's package is AGPL, BRIA RMBG 2.0 requires a commercial agreement for commercial deployment, and even BiRefNet's maintainer has publicly discussed licensing complications caused by training datasets. citeturn536294search3turn914894search8turn536294search7

For the MVP, the lowest-risk setup is therefore:

**Development:** configurable background-removal provider adapter.

**Production option A:** licensed commercial API.

**Production option B:** self-hosted model only after written license review.

**Fallback behavior:** if no valid provider is configured, clearly display “Background removal service not configured” rather than generating a fake cutout.

