## Deployment checklist

Google AI Studio can currently publish a generated full-stack app to Cloud Run directly, and standard deployment requires an associated Google Cloud project with billing unless the account qualifies for the limited Starter Tier. AI Studio also supports linking the project to GitHub for two-way source synchronization. citeturn696138search3turn430749search0

Use this deployment sequence:

1. **Create the AI Studio project.** Paste the Master Prompt into Build mode, select a web app, allow it to generate the full-stack React/Node project, and fix every build/test failure before publication. AI Studio's web environment supports a React client and Node.js server component. citeturn430749search0turn696138search4

2. **Connect GitHub immediately.** Create or link a repository through AI Studio Settings, then verify that `.env`, service-account credentials, API keys, and other secrets are excluded from source control. AI Studio currently supports push/pull synchronization with GitHub. citeturn430749search0

3. **Enable Firebase Authentication and Firestore.** Configure only the owner's login initially. Start Firestore rules in locked/production mode and explicitly grant public reads only for intentionally published CMS content; Firebase recommends deny-by-default rules and testing rules with the Emulator Suite. citeturn696138search6turn696138search5

4. **Configure background-removal processing.** Choose either a commercially licensed API or a model whose code, weights, datasets, and commercial-use conditions have been reviewed. Do not assume the license of a wrapper such as `rembg` covers every model it can download. citeturn914894search5turn914894search7

5. **Configure the PDF backend.** Test compression against text-heavy, scanned, mixed-image, malformed, encrypted, and large PDFs. File uploads should use defense-in-depth controls rather than trusting only extension or MIME headers. citeturn536294search9

6. **Set Cloud Run limits.** Configure memory, CPU, timeouts, maximum instances, concurrency, environment variables, and service-account permissions. A public website can be deployed as a public Cloud Run service; Google documents source/container deployment and public invocation configuration. citeturn696138search7

7. **Create billing alerts.** Google Cloud budgets can generate actual/forecast spend alerts, but an alerts-only budget does not itself cap spending, so maximum instances and provider quotas remain important. citeturn430749search4

8. **Connect the domain.** For a production custom domain, Google's current preferred Cloud Run option is a global external Application Load Balancer; Firebase Hosting is another documented option, while direct Cloud Run domain mapping has more limited availability. citeturn430749search3

9. **Enable HTTPS, canonical host, Search Console and sitemap.** Put `sitemap.xml` at the site root and submit it through Search Console. Google recommends absolute canonical URLs in sitemaps. citeturn817648search5

10. **Enable GA4 after privacy configuration.** GA4 supports automatically collected, enhanced measurement, recommended, and custom events; RankNova should use custom tool events without sending document contents or QR payloads. citeturn817648search2

11. **Configure a CMP before relevant AdSense traffic.** Google's current publisher requirements require a Google-certified CMP integrated with the IAB TCF for personalized ads served in the EEA, UK and Switzerland. citeturn817648search0turn817648search1

12. **Apply to AdSense only after the site is genuinely complete.** Keep the ad system disabled until your account/site configuration is approved. Compliance with published policies is necessary, but approval or future earnings should never be represented as guaranteed. Google may disable ad serving/accounts for policy violations. citeturn696138search0

For Firestore business data, scheduled backups are available but require the Blaze plan; managed exports/imports also require billing. Backups should cover CMS/settings—not transient user PDFs/images. citeturn430749search1turn430749search2

